+        },
+        {
+          "file_path": "plugins/pretty-link/app/controllers/PrliAuthenticatorController.php",
+          "line_number": 109,
+          "note": "All writes target fixed plugin-owned option names such as `prli_authenticator_account_email` and `prli_authenticator_secret_token`."
+        }
+      ],
+      "reasoning": {
+        "hypothesis": "Phase 1 and Semgrep suggested that `process_connect()` may let an attacker drive SSRF through `site_uuid` or perform privilege escalation through `update_option()`.",
+        "trace": "The init handler `PrliAuthenticatorController::process_connect()` reads `$_GET['site_uuid']` and `$_GET['auth_code']`, sanitizes both, sends a request to `PRLI_AUTH_SERVICE_URL . \"/api/tokens/{$site_uuid}\"`, decodes the response, and stores only fixed Pretty Links connection options.",
+        "defenses": "The code requires a valid nonce at `PrliAuthenticatorController.php:83`, then requires `PrliUtils::is_authorized()` at line 88. The sink writes are hard-coded plugin option keys, not user-controlled option names.",
